* START-HISTORY:
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - the script directory is SDSYS pstmp, lower case on
*           disk; sd.iss hands secure-psdir.ps1 the same name.  5.12 (a).
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - written for the OS account commands
* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - an elevated session sends the script to its helper
* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - scripts go in SDSYS\PSTMP, not the account
*                      directory, where every SD user could read the password
*                      in a !set_passwd script and rewrite any script before
*                      the elevated helper ran it
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    rc = !ps_script(script)
*
*    script - PowerShell text.  Lines are field marks.
*
*    WHY THIS EXISTS.  Creating a Windows account needs a password, and the
*    obvious "net user <name> <password> /add" puts that password in a process
*    command line, where any local user can read it through Task Manager,
*    Get-CimInstance Win32_Process or ETW.  PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8
*    rejected exactly that pattern for batch login and the same reasoning
*    applies here.  Decision from the repository owner, 14 Aug 2026: write the
*    script to a file instead, run it, delete it.
*
*    IT GOES IN SDSYS\PSTMP, AND THE PARAGRAPH THAT USED TO BE HERE WAS
*    WRONG - corrected 16 Aug 2026, having been believed since 14 Aug.  It
*    said the script was safe in the account directory because the installer
*    breaks inheritance on C:\ProgramData\SD and "grants narrowly", so the
*    file was "protected by work already done, with no icacls call of its
*    own".  The grant is narrow against the WORLD and not against SD's own
*    users: it is sdusers:(OI)(CI)(M), inherited by every file underneath, so
*    each script was readable AND WRITABLE by every other SD user.  Reading it
*    yields !set_passwd's new password in clear; rewriting it between this
*    write and the helper's execution gets arbitrary content run with full
*    privilege.  The second is a local privilege escalation and is the reason
*    for the change.
*
*    PSTMP is created by the installer through gplbld/secure-psdir.ps1, whose
*    header carries the ACL and why it has the shape it does.  In short: SD
*    users may create files there and nothing more, CREATOR OWNER gives each
*    script to the session that wrote it, and Administrators keep full control
*    so the elevated helper can read what it is told to run.  This routine
*    FAILS CLOSED if the directory is absent rather than falling back to the
*    account directory.
*
*    A FULL WINDOWS PATHNAME, which is now possible and was not on 14 Aug.
*    The paragraph replaced here explained a relative name as a way round
*    BASIC being unable to make one, OS$FULLPATH returning POSIX.  @sdsys is a
*    Windows pathname already - sd.conf carries SDSYS=C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys,
*    and @ds is the backslash - so @sdsys:@ds:'PSTMP':@ds:id is a name both
*    PowerShell and the helper open as it stands, from any working directory.
*
*    Invoke-Expression rather than "& .\script.ps1", for two reasons measured
*    on 14 Aug 2026: the ampersand form does not propagate the script's exit
*    status - a script ending "exit 7" gave 1 - and running a .ps1 file is
*    subject to the execution policy, while running text is not.  So this
*    needs no -ExecutionPolicy Bypass anywhere.
*
*    THE FILE IS DELETED WHETHER THE SCRIPT SUCCEEDED OR NOT.  If SD dies
*    between the write and the delete, a script holding a password is left in
*    SDSYS\PSTMP; it is named $PS.TMP.<user number> so it is recognisable, and
*    the directory's ACL is what protects it in the meantime - it is readable
*    only by the user who wrote it and by administrators.  secure-psdir.ps1
*    clears the directory on every install for the same reason.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function ps_script(script)
$catalog !ps_script

$include int$keys.h

* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - the session's elevated helper, when it has one.
deffun elevate(action, arg) calling '!elevate'

   set.status 0
   rc = -1

   if script = '' then
      set.status 1
      return rc
   end

   id = '$PS.TMP.' : @userno

* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - NOT THE ACCOUNT DIRECTORY, WHICH EVERY SD USER CAN
*   WRITE.  This used to be openpath '.', so an SDSYS session wrote its scripts
*   into C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys, where the data tree's sdusers:Modify is
*   inherited by every file - checked on the installed tree, 16 Aug 2026.
*
*   The disclosure was the obvious half: !set_passwd's script carries a new
*   Windows password in clear until it is deleted a moment later.  THE SERIOUS
*   HALF IS ELEVATION: any SD user could rewrite another user's pending script
*   between this write and the helper executing it, and the helper runs what it
*   finds with full privilege.  PSTMP is created by the installer
*   (gplbld/secure-psdir.ps1) so that each script is owned by, and readable
*   only by, the session that wrote it - plus Administrators, which is how the
*   helper reads it.
*
*   NO K$WINPATH HERE, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS SHORTER THAN IT WAS.  @sdsys is
*   already a Windows pathname - sd.conf carries "SDSYS=C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys"
*   and @ds is the backslash - so the name below is one PowerShell can open as
*   it stands.  The ospath/K$WINPATH pair this replaces existed only to convert
*   a POSIX working directory.
   ps.dir = @sdsys : @ds : 'pstmp'
   ps.path = ps.dir : @ds : id

*  FAIL CLOSED IF IT IS MISSING.  An install that predates this step, or one
*  where secure-psdir.ps1 did not run, must not silently fall back to writing
*  privileged scripts somewhere every SD user can edit them.
   openpath ps.dir to sys.f else
      set.status 1
      return rc
   end

   write script to sys.f, id on error
      set.status 1
      return rc
   end

*  SH1 names PowerShell, so the command IS PowerShell.  Single quotes, so the
*  leading $ of the name is literal to PowerShell.
   q = char(39)

* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - AN ELEVATED SESSION SENDS THE SCRIPT TO ITS HELPER
*   INSTEAD OF RUNNING IT HERE.  PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 4.  Entering SDSYS
*   obtains privilege for the session (CPROC, !elevate) and this is where the
*   privileged half of CREATE.ACCOUNT actually uses it - !create_user,
*   !set_passwd and !os_group all arrive through here.
*
*   ASKED ONLY WHEN THE SESSION HAS ADMINISTRATOR RIGHTS, so an ordinary
*   session pays nothing: the test is a flag read, not a process.  A session
*   that is genuinely elevated already - the installer's own account step, a
*   bootstrap, "sd -internal" - has no helper, gets 9 back and runs the script
*   itself, which is the behaviour this file has always had.
*
*   THE HELPER NEEDS A WINDOWS PATHNAME, and ps.path already is one - it is
*   built from @sdsys.  It is a separate process and does not inherit this
*   working directory, so a relative name would mean nothing to it; that is
*   what the conversion removed above used to be for.
   if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then
      rc = elevate('RUN', ps.path)
*     9 is "no helper for this session", and is the only answer that
*     means "not handled" rather than a result.  Anything else, success
*     or failure, is the script's own and is returned as it stands.
      if rc # 9 then
         delete sys.f, id
         close sys.f
         return rc
      end
   end

*  A FULL PATHNAME NOW, not a name relative to the working directory: the
*  script no longer lives in the account directory the child inherits.  Single
*  quotes still, so the leading $ of the name stays literal to PowerShell.
   os.execute 'Get-Content -LiteralPath ' : q : ps.path : q : ' -Raw | Invoke-Expression'
   rc = os.error()

   delete sys.f, id
   close sys.f

return rc
end

* END-CODE
